            <ArticleTitle>Prenatal vitamin use and vitamin D status during pregnancy, differences by race and overweight status.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/jp.2014.198</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We aimed to study whether prenatal vitamin (PNV) use protects against low 25-hydroxyvitamin D (25[OH]D) levels in all women and particularly in obese and black women who are both at risk of vitamin D deficiency and poor pregnancy outcomes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studied 1019 women enrolled in a prospective study at Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston, from 2007 to 2009. We used multivariable logistic regression to analyze associations of PNV use and odds of vitamin D deficiency defined as 25[OH]D levels &lt;50 nmol l(-1).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In all, 56% of black and 86% of white women reported pre- and/or postconceptional PNV use. In the first trimester, 75% of black and 19% of white women were vitamin D deficient. Lack of PNV use among black women was not associated with vitamin D deficiency (adjusted odds ratio (OR) 1.0, 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.4, 2.3) but was among white women (OR 3.5, 95% CI 2.1, 5.8) (interaction P&lt;0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Ongoing trials of vitamin D supplementation during pregnancy should consider potential effect modification by race/ethnicity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
